diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..bf309eb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,126 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Bots are implemented as users with a `Bot` STI subclass (`users.type = "Bot"`). This
+lets them use the existing membership, message, avatar, and broadcast plumbing: bots can
+be granted access to rooms, including closed rooms from the room settings UI, and author
+normal `Message` records.
+
+Unlike people, bots do not automatically join every open room. A bot author can either
+add a bot to specific rooms or enable **Add to all open rooms** on the bot form. When that
+switch is on, the bot joins every open room that exists at the time, as well as every
+open room created later. Turning the switch off leaves the bot's current memberships in
+place.
+
+## Bot credentials
+
+Each bot gets a `bot_code` from `has_secure_token`, stored in `users.bot_code` with a
+unique index. The code is shown only on the bots management page. Bot codes are accepted
+by the unauthenticated, CSRF-exempt `POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages` endpoint.
+The bot must be a member of the room.
+
+The request body is read with `request.raw_post` and used verbatim as the message body;
+there is no JSON envelope or field-name parsing. Blank bodies are rejected with 422.
+Successful requests return 201 and a `Location: /messages/:id` header. Invalid codes
+return 401 and unknown/inaccessible rooms return 404.
+
+Messages created through the API call the same `broadcast_create` and model callbacks as
+messages created in the UI, so connected clients and web push notifications behave
+identically.
+
+## Management
+
+Any signed-in member can create bots from `/bots`; non-administrators see only bots they
+created, while administrators see all bots. Bots have an owner (`users.creator_id`),
+the standard user avatar attachment, and an optional `webhook_url`. Deleting a bot
+destroys the record, so its code can no longer authenticate.
+
+Administrators can configure **Bots per member** on the account settings page. The
+setting defaults to 5 and is stored in `accounts.settings`. Members can create up to
+the configured number of live bots. Administrators are exempt from the limit. At the
+limit, the bots page hides the add link and shows a limit message. Creation is also
+blocked in the controller and by a model validation, so direct requests cannot bypass
+the limit. Lowering the setting leaves existing bots in place; it only prevents new
+ones from being created. Deleted bots no longer count toward the limit.
+
+## Webhooks
+
+Bots may optionally have a `webhook_url`, set from the bot form. Leaving the field
+blank clears the URL. Campfire enqueues `Bot::WebhookJob` after the relevant record
+commits, so posting a message or changing room membership never waits for the bot's
+HTTP endpoint.
+
+### Mention events
+
+When a message mentions a bot that has a webhook, the job POSTs a JSON `mention` event
+containing the author, room (including the bot's `reply_to` posting path), and message
+details.
+
+### Joined events
+
+Whenever a bot is added to a non-direct room--whether automatically through **Add to
+all open rooms** or explicitly by a person--the job POSTs a JSON `joined` event exactly
+once for that room:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/42-Xy3abc9defGh/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+Direct conversations never trigger `joined`.
+
+### Replies
+
+For both event types, a `200` response with a `text/plain` or `text/html` body is posted
+back to the room as a message from the bot. A `204` response (and any other status or
+content type) produces no reply. The job broadcasts replies through the same message
+plumbing as any other message, so they appear live in the room and trigger the normal
+unread and push notification flow.
+
+## Abuse protection
+
+Bot safety state is stored on the bot's `users` row so it survives process restarts and
+applies across every room:
+
+- `suspended_at` / `suspension_reason` indicate that a bot has been stopped.
+- `rate_limited_until` / `rate_limit_attempts` track the current flood cooldown.
+- `consecutive_webhook_failures` tracks webhook delivery health.
+
+The limits are fixed in `Bot` and are deliberately not account settings.
+
+### Flooding
+
+A bot may create at most ten messages in any rolling ten-second window. API posts and
+webhook replies both use the same guard in `Bot#message_posting_status`, and both count
+because they create normal bot-authored `Message` records.
+
+The first attempt beyond the limit is rejected without creating a message and starts a
+30-second cooldown. While the cooldown is active:
+
+- `POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages` returns `429 Too Many Requests` with a
+  `Retry-After` header containing the remaining whole seconds.
+- Webhook replies are not written to the room.
+
+Each blocked attempt is recorded against the same cooldown. The fifth blocked attempt
+suspends the bot; the request that causes that suspension still returns 429, and
+subsequent requests return 403. Once the cooldown expires, posting is allowed again if
+the rolling message window has room.
+
+### Webhook failures
+
+A webhook delivery succeeds only for a 2xx response. Any other HTTP response, a timeout,
+or a connection error is a failure. Ten consecutive failures suspend the bot, and any
+successful delivery resets the consecutive-failure counter.
+
+### Suspension and reactivation
+
+Suspension only sets the safety state; it does not remove memberships, messages, or the
+bot record. Suspended bots therefore remain on the bots page, continue to count toward
+their owner's bot limit, return `403 Forbidden` from the posting endpoint, and are
+excluded from webhook delivery.
+
+Administrators see a **Reactivate** button next to each suspended bot. The button uses
+the browser's native confirmation dialog and calls `PATCH /bots/:id/reactivate`.
+Reactivation clears the suspension and safety counters. Marking a bot suspended is
+automatic; reactivation is restricted to administrators.
